<!-- ===== Task Set One: Brainstorm (slides 5-10) ===== -->
<Vertical id="hs_ts1_screen" title="Task Set One: Brainstorm">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Task Set One: Brainstorm

### What causes people to experience homelessness?
]]></Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="hs_cause_indiv_q">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
**Type a cause that focuses on an individual-level factor:**
]]></Markdown>
<TextArea id="hs_cause_indiv" rows="3" placeholder="Cause 1…" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="hs_cause_struct_q">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
**Type a structural cause:**
]]></Markdown>
<TextArea id="hs_cause_struct" rows="3" placeholder="Cause 2…" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="hs_causes_list" title="Our reasons">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Why Do People Experience Homelessness?

- In some cities, there is not enough affordable housing available for low- and middle-income residents, which increases the risk of housing loss.
- When people lose their jobs or experience unstable employment and do not have financial savings, they may be unable to keep up with rent or mortgage payments.
- Mental health conditions or substance use challenges can make it more difficult for individuals to maintain steady employment or manage housing responsibilities.
- When wages remain low while rents continue to rise, even people who are working full time may struggle to afford stable housing.
- People experiencing domestic violence may leave their homes quickly for safety reasons and have limited immediate housing options.
- Racial discrimination in housing and lending markets can limit access to stable and affordable housing for some groups.
- Limited access to cash assistance or income support programs may leave vulnerable individuals and families without a financial safety net during crises.
- People who are unsheltered may face increased risks from extreme heat or severe weather, which can worsen health conditions and housing instability.
]]></Markdown>
<Markdown>- {{@hs_cause_indiv.value}}&#10;- {{@hs_cause_struct.value}}</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<!-- ===== Task Set Two: Review (slides 11-16) ===== -->
<Vertical id="hs_fg_hl_screen" title="Focus group transcript">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Comm360 Community Focus Group: Transcript Excerpt
]]></Markdown>
<TextSelectionInput id="hs_fg_hl" src="readings/focus_group_highlight.textSelectionpeg" separatorRegexp="§" separatorHidden="true" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="hs_fg_ty" title="Ty's highlights">
<Markdown id="hs_fg_ty_md" src="readings/focus_group_ty.md" />
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="hs_reasons_list" title="All the reasons">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Why Do People Experience Homelessness?

- Trauma people experience — domestic violence, family conflict, untreated mental health conditions–can leave them with disrupted employment, relationships, and stability leading to housing insecurity.
- Records such as eviction filing and other minor chargers often appear in tenant screening databases. Many landlords automatically deny applications with prior eviction history.
- In some places, limited economic opportunity — such as fewer job opportunities or restrictive labor market conditions — can make it harder for people to find and maintain stable employment, increasing the risk of homelessness.
- In some cities, there is not enough affordable housing available for low- and middle-income residents, which increases the risk of housing loss.
- When people lose their jobs or experience unstable employment and do not have financial savings, they may be unable to keep up with rent or mortgage payments.
- Substance use challenges can make it more difficult for individuals to maintain steady employment or manage housing responsibilities.
- When wages remain low while rents continue to rise, even people who are working full time may struggle to afford stable housing.
- Racial discrimination in housing and lending markets can limit access to stable and affordable housing for some groups.
- Limited access to cash assistance or income support programs may leave vulnerable individuals and families without a financial safety net during crises.
- People who are unsheltered may face increased risks from extreme heat or severe weather, which can worsen health conditions and housing instability.
]]></Markdown>
<Markdown>- {{@hs_cause_indiv.value}}&#10;- {{@hs_cause_struct.value}}</Markdown>
</Vertical>

