<Vertical id="hs_polisci_table1" title="Comparison table">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Disciplinary Perspective Comparison Table

*Tip: use the full-screen button to see the whole table.*
]]></Markdown>
<Markdown id="hs_polisci_table1_md" popout="fullscreen"><![CDATA[
| **Element of Disciplinary Perspective** | **Economics (Cebula & Saunoris, 2021)** | **Public Policy (Parolin, 2021)** | **Psychology / Psychiatry Barry et al. (2024)** | **Real Estate Economics (Contat et al., 2024)** | **Political Science (Fowle, 2022)** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Phenomena studied** | Labor markets, entrepreneurship, economic opportunity, employment | Income support policies, public assistance programs, housing stability | Mental health disorders, substance use disorders, psychiatric conditions among individuals experiencing homelessness | Climate risks, housing markets, property values, affordability, and displacement | Structural racism, institutional inequality, housing systems, and homelessness |
| **Questions asked** | How do economic conditions influence housing insecurity? | How do income support policies influence family homelessness? | What role do mental health disorders play in understanding homelessness? | How do climate risks influence housing markets and housing stability? | ??? |
| **Evidence used** | State-level economic indicators, labor market data, entrepreneurship measures, homelessness rates | Administrative data on student homelessness and state policy data | Published studies reporting mental health diagnoses among homeless populations (85 studies; 48,414 participants) | Housing market data, climate-risk maps, insurance, mortgage, and migration data | ??? |
| **Methods** | Econometric analysis of state-level panel data | Statistical analysis of administrative and policy data. | Systematic review and meta-analysis | Literature review synthesizing quantitative and qualitative research | ??? |
| **Underlying Assumptions** | Economic systems, incentives, and institutional conditions influence human behavior and social outcomes. | Government policies and institutions shape social conditions and people's opportunities. | Human thoughts, emotions, behaviors, and mental processes influence people's experiences and life outcomes. | Housing systems are shaped by interactions among market forces, the built environment, and the natural environment. | ??? |
| **Main insight** | **Higher levels of entrepreneurial activity and labor market freedom may reduce homelessness by expanding economic opportunity and reducing barriers to employment.** | **Public policy design, particularly access to direct cash assistance, can play an important role in reducing family homelessness.** | **Mental health disorders are highly prevalent among individuals experiencing homelessness, highlighting the need for coordinated housing, mental health, and substance use services.** | **Climate-related environmental risks can influence housing markets, property values, housing affordability, and displacement patterns, disproportionately affecting economically vulnerable populations.** | **Homelessness in the United States is shaped by structural racism embedded in housing, economic, and political systems, leading to persistent racial disparities in homelessness.** |
]]></Markdown>
</Vertical>

